1 FCALC

   A scientific calculator: numbers, complex values, vectors and
   matrices, the functions of a scientific calculator and some of a
   statistician's, functions of your own, and commands that solve
   equations, integrate, differentiate, find a polynomial's roots, and
   show values in tables and graphs.

   fcalc is a Fortran 90 program, and govax's example of a whole
   program: its sources are in SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSHLP.EXAMPLES.FCALC].
   The supplied SYLOGIN.COM defines FCALC as a foreign command for
   SYS$SYSTEM:FCALC.EXE.

   Format

     FCALC [line]

   With a line, fcalc runs it and ends: "FCALC 2^10 + 1" prints 1025.
   Without one, fcalc reads lines at its prompt, "fcalc> ", until EXIT,
   QUIT, or end of file (Ctrl/Z). Inside fcalc, HELP shows this text, and
   HELP topic shows a topic below it ("HELP COMMANDS SOLVE").

   Each line is an expression, whose value fcalc shows and keeps as ans;
   an assignment, name = expression; a definition of a function,
   name(x, ...) = expression; or a command. Case doesn't matter, a line
   ending in ; is quiet (the value is kept, not shown), and a line
   starting with ! is a comment.

2 Expressions

   Numbers are written as in Fortran or C: 42, 1.5e-3, .5. A number
   followed by i is imaginary: 2i, 1.5e3i. Whole numbers may also be
   written in hexadecimal (0xFF), octal (0o377), or binary (0b1011).

   Every value is complex. One with no imaginary part is shown as a real
   number, and a whole number with no decimal point:

     fcalc> (1 + 2i) * (3 - i)
     5 + 5i

   The constants are pi, e, i, eps (the machine's epsilon, the distance
   from 1 to the next larger number), and huge (the largest number fcalc
   keeps, 1e300). ans is the last value shown.

   fcalc never stops on an arithmetic error: a division by zero, a result
   too large, or a function's argument out of its range is a message, and
   the line has no value. See Messages.

2 Operators

   From the loosest to the tightest:

     or                    either is true (not zero)
     and                   both are true
     not                   true if the operand is false
     ==  <>  <  <=  >  >=  comparisons, of the real parts; true is 1,
                           false 0; == and <> are to within 64 epsilons
     +  -                  addition, subtraction
     *  /  mod             multiplication, division, remainder
     -  +                  sign
     ^  **                 power, right to left: 2^3^2 is 2^9
     !                     factorial, after its operand: 5! is 120

   Parentheses group. -2^2 is -4, as in mathematics. A power of a
   negative or complex number, and a fractional power, is the principal
   value: (-8)^(1/3) is 1 + 1.73205080757i.

   On vectors and matrices + and - work element by element, and * is the
   matrix product; .*, ./, and .^ work element by element, ' is the
   conjugate transpose, .' the transpose, and A \ b solves A*x = b. See
   Vectors_and_matrices.

2 Variables

   A name is a letter followed by letters, digits, and underscores, up to
   31 characters; case doesn't matter. An assignment shows the value:

     fcalc> r = 2.5
     r = 2.5
     fcalc> area = pi * r^2
     area = 19.6349540849

   A name must be given a value before it is used, and may not be one of
   the constants, a built-in function's name, or one of the words or,
   and, not, mod, solve, integrate, diff, table, and plot.

   An element or a section of a vector or matrix may be assigned:
   v(2) = 0, m(1, :) = [1, 2].

   LIST VARIABLES shows the variables and their values, and CLEAR name
   (or CLEAR ALL) forgets them.

2 User_functions

   A function of up to 8 parameters is defined by a line naming it with
   its parameters, an = sign, and its value, and fcalc shows the
   definition as it keeps it:

     fcalc> f(x) = x^3 - 2*x - 5
     f(x) = x^3 - 2*x - 5
     fcalc> g(x, y) = sqrt(x^2 + y^2)
     g(x, y) = sqrt(x^2 + y^2)
     fcalc> f(2)
     -1
     fcalc> g(3, 4)
     5

   The expression may use the parameters, the variables (their values
   when the function is called, not when it was defined), the built-in
   functions, and other functions of your own, defined before or after.

   A function may call itself, through if(), whose arms are evaluated
   only when chosen:

     fcalc> fact(n) = if(n < 2, 1, n * fact(n - 1))
     fact(n) = if(n < 2, 1, n * fact(n - 1))
     fcalc> fact(10)
     3628800

   Calls may go 200 deep. Defining a function again replaces it. LIST
   FUNCTIONS shows the definitions, and CLEAR name forgets one.

2 Functions

   The built-in functions are listed by kind in the topics below. Every
   function of one argument works on each element of a vector or matrix;
   the list functions also take a vector or matrix as their list.
   Functions of an angle use the angle mode (SET ANGLE).

3 Elementary

     sqrt(x)      square root           cbrt(x)       cube root
     exp(x)       e to the power x      ln(x)         natural logarithm
     log10(x)     common logarithm      log2(x)       base 2 logarithm
     log(x, b)    logarithm to base b   hypot(x, y)   sqrt(x^2 + y^2)

   Each takes a complex argument and gives the principal value: sqrt(-4)
   is 2i, ln(-1) is pi*i (3.14159265359i).

3 Trigonometric

     sin(x)   cos(x)   tan(x)   sec(x)   csc(x)   cot(x)
     asin(x)  acos(x)  atan(x)  atan2(y, x)
     sinh(x)  cosh(x)  tanh(x)  asinh(x) acosh(x) atanh(x)

   The trigonometric functions take and give angles in the angle mode,
   radians, degrees, or grads (SET ANGLE); the hyperbolic functions
   don't depend on it. atan2(y, x) is the angle of the point (x, y), from
   -pi to pi. Every one takes complex arguments: asin(2) is
   1.57079632679 - 1.31695789692i.

3 Complex

     re(z)        real part             im(z)         imaginary part
     abs(z)       magnitude             arg(z)        angle, -pi to pi
     conj(z)      complex conjugate
     polar(r, t)  r * (cos(t) + i*sin(t)), from a magnitude and an angle
     cis(t)       cos(t) + i*sin(t)

   arg, polar, and cis use the angle mode.

3 Integers

     floor(x)     the largest whole number not above x
     ceil(x)      the smallest whole number not below x
     round(x)     the nearest whole number, halves away from zero
     trunc(x)     x with its fraction dropped
     frac(x)      x - trunc(x)
     sign(x)      -1, 0, or 1
     gcd(a, b, ...), lcm(a, b, ...)   greatest common divisor, least
                                      common multiple
     isprime(n)   1 if n is prime, 0 if not
     ncr(n, k)    combinations, n! / (k! (n-k)!)
     npr(n, k)    permutations, n! / (n-k)!

   gcd, lcm, isprime, ncr, and npr need whole numbers.

3 Special

     gamma(x)     the gamma function; gamma(n + 1) is n!
     lgamma(x)    the logarithm of abs(gamma(x))
     erf(x)       the error function
     erfc(x)      1 - erf(x), accurate where erf(x) is near 1
     beta(a, b)   gamma(a) gamma(b) / gamma(a + b)

   These take real arguments. x! is gamma(x + 1), so 0.5! is
   0.886226925453.

3 Lists

     min(...)     max(...)     the smallest and largest
     sum(...)     prod(...)    the sum and product
     mean(...)    median(...)  the mean and median
     var(...)     stdev(...)   the sample variance and standard deviation
                               (at least two values)

   Each takes any number of arguments, or a vector or matrix whose
   elements are the list: mean(1, 2, 3, 4) and mean([1, 2, 3, 4]) are both
   2.5. min and max compare real parts.

3 Arrays

     size(a)  size(a, d)  rows(a)  cols(a)  numel(a)
     zeros(m, n)  ones(m, n)  eye(n)  diag(v)  linspace(a, b, n)
     range(a, b)  range(a, b, step)  reshape(a, m, n)
     transpose(a)  trace(m)  det(m)  inv(m)  rank(m)  eig(m)
     norm(v)  norm(v, p)  dot(u, v)  cross(u, v)  kron(a, b)
     cumsum(v)  sort(v)  roots(c)  poly(r)  polyval(c, x)

   See Vectors_and_matrices Functions for what each does.

3 Other

     if(c, a, b)  a if c is true (not zero), else b; only the arm chosen
                  is evaluated, so a function may call itself through it
     rand()       a random number from 0 to 1
     rand(a, b)   a random number from a to b

   SET SEED n starts rand's sequence again from n.

2 Vectors_and_matrices

   A value may be a vector or a matrix of complex numbers. Brackets make
   one: commas separate the elements of a row, and semicolons the rows.

     [1, 2, 3]          a row vector
     [1; 2; 3]          a column vector
     [1, 2; 3, 4]       a 2 by 2 matrix
     [v, 4]  [m; v]     joined: the parts must fit

   Elements may be any expressions. A matrix is shown in rows, its
   columns aligned, each element to the digits of the largest:

     fcalc> m = [2, 1; 1, 3]
     m =
       2  1
       1  3

   Arrays may hold up to 10,000 elements. See the topics below.

3 Indexing

   v(k) is a vector's kth element, and m(i, j) a matrix's element in row
   i and column j, counting from 1. An index may be a range, a:b, or :
   for all, or a vector of indexes:

     m(2, :)          the second row
     m(:, 1)          the first column
     v(2:4)           the second to fourth elements
     v([3, 1])        the third and the first

   end isn't a word here: use size, rows, or cols (v(numel(v))).

   Assigning to an element or a section changes it: v(1) = 10,
   m(:, 2) = [0; 0].

3 Operators

     a + b, a - b     element by element; a scalar is spread over an array
     a * b            the matrix product; with a scalar, element by element
     a .* b, a ./ b   element by element
     a .^ b           element by element power
     m ^ n            a square matrix to a whole power; a negative power
                      through the inverse
     a'               the conjugate transpose
     a.'              the transpose
     A \ b            the solution x of A*x = b
     comparisons      element by element, giving 1s and 0s

   Shapes must agree: a 2 by 3 matrix plus a 3 by 2 one is NOTCONFORM.

3 Functions

     size(a), size(a, d)   [rows, columns], or the size of dimension d
     rows(a), cols(a), numel(a)   rows, columns, elements
     zeros(m, n), ones(m, n), eye(n)   arrays of 0s and 1s, the identity
     diag(v)               a square matrix with v down its diagonal, or a
                           square matrix's diagonal as a vector
     linspace(a, b, n)     n values from a to b, equally spaced
     range(a, b[, s])      a, a+s, ... up to b (s is 1 if not given)
     reshape(a, m, n)      a's elements, column by column, as m by n
     cumsum(v), sort(v)    running sums; sorted by real part
     dot(u, v), cross(u, v), kron(a, b)   products
     norm(v[, p])          the p-norm (2 if not given)
     transpose(a), trace(m), det(m), inv(m), rank(m)
     eig(m)                the eigenvalues, sorted, by the QR method
     roots(c), poly(r), polyval(c, x)   see Commands ROOTS

2 Commands

   A line that starts with one of these words is a command. Each topic
   below describes one. RUN, LOAD, SAVE, LOG, and HELP followed by = or
   ( aren't commands: RUN = 3 assigns a variable, and LOG(8, 2) is a
   logarithm.

3 SOLVE

   Finds a root of an equation.

     SOLVE expr [= expr] FOR x [FROM a [TO b]]

   x is a name standing for the unknown; a variable of that name is left
   alone. With FROM a alone, fcalc starts the secant method at a (at 0 if
   FROM is left out), and from a complex start can find complex roots.
   With FROM a TO b, the expression must change sign between a and b,
   and fcalc narrows the bracket by Brent's method, which always
   converges.

     fcalc> solve x^2 = 2 for x from 1
     x = 1.41421356237
     fcalc> solve cos(t) = t for t from 0 to 1
     t = 0.739085133215

   NOROOT if the secant method finds none; SAMESIGN if the bracket's ends
   have values of the same sign.

3 INTEGRATE

   Integrates an expression between two limits.

     INTEGRATE expr FOR x FROM a TO b

   fcalc uses adaptive Gauss-Kronrod quadrature: each piece of the
   interval is integrated by rules of 7 and 15 points, whose difference
   estimates the error, and the piece with the largest error is cut in
   two, until the pieces' errors add up to little enough. The pieces
   shrink where the expression is hard to integrate, so a jump, or a
   singularity at an end that has a finite integral, is handled too.

     fcalc> integrate exp(-x^2) for x from 0 to 3
     0.88620734826

   If the pieces (200) run out before the estimate is small, the value comes
   with %FCALC-W-INACCURATE, naming how large the error may be.

3 DIFF

   Differentiates an expression at a point.

     DIFF expr FOR x AT a

   fcalc uses Ridders' method, Richardson extrapolation of central
   differences, which gives about 10 digits for a smooth function. a may
   be complex.

     fcalc> diff sin(x) for x at 0
     1

   At a point where the function isn't smooth, the value comes with
   %FCALC-W-INACCURATE.

3 ROOTS

   Finds every root, real and complex, of a polynomial.

     ROOTS c1, c2, ..., cn
     ROOTS v

   The coefficients are given from the highest power down, or as a
   vector: ROOTS 1, 0, -1 is x^2 - 1. fcalc uses the Durand-Kerner
   method, then polishes each root by Newton's method, and shows the
   roots as a column, kept as ans:

     fcalc> roots 1, -6, 11, -6
       1
       2
       3

   The functions roots(c) (the same, as a value), poly(r) (the
   coefficients of the polynomial with roots r), and polyval(c, x) (its
   value at x) go with it.

3 TABLE

   Shows expressions' values in columns.

     TABLE expr[, expr...] FOR x FROM a TO b [STEP s]
     TABLE v[, w...]

   With FOR, each expression is evaluated at a, a + s, ... up to b (s is
   (b - a)/10 if STEP is left out), a row for each point, up to 1,000. A
   point where an expression has no value shows the error's name,
   DIVZERO, say. Without FOR, each expression is a vector, all of one
   length, shown side by side. Up to 8 expressions. See Examples
   Table_and_plot.

3 PLOT

   Draws expressions' values as a graph of characters.

     PLOT expr[, expr...] FOR x FROM a TO b [SIZE w, h] [Y lo, hi]
     PLOT v[, w...] [VERSUS u] [SIZE w, h] [Y lo, hi]

   With FOR, each expression is evaluated at as many points as the graph
   is wide. Without FOR, each expression is a vector, drawn against its
   index, or, after VERSUS, against the vector u.

   The graph is 61 characters wide and 19 high unless SIZE says otherwise
   (10 to 100 wide, 3 to 60 high). Its vertical range is the values'
   unless Y gives one; values outside it aren't drawn. Each curve has a
   mark, *, o, #, x, @, %, &, and $ in turn, named under the graph. The
   axes are drawn where zero is in range. Points where a value can't be
   computed, or isn't real, are left out. See Examples Table_and_plot.

3 LIST

     LIST [VARIABLES | FUNCTIONS | ALL]

   Shows the variables and their values, the functions and their
   definitions, or both (ALL, the default), in the order of their names.

3 CLEAR

     CLEAR name ...
     CLEAR ALL

   Forgets the variables and functions named, or every one. A name not
   defined is %FCALC-W-NOTDEF.

3 SET

     SET DIGITS n
     SET FORMAT AUTO | FIXED | SCIENTIFIC | ENGINEERING
     SET BASE DECIMAL | HEX | OCTAL | BINARY
     SET ANGLE RADIANS | DEGREES | GRADS
     SET SEED n
     SET ECHO | NOECHO

   DIGITS, FORMAT, and BASE choose how values are shown (see Display).
   ANGLE is the unit the trigonometric functions use (radians unless
   set). SEED starts the random numbers again from n, so a sequence can
   be repeated. ECHO shows each line read after the prompt, as a terminal
   does, which makes a transcript of a file of commands (NOECHO unless
   set).

3 SHOW

     SHOW SETTINGS
     SHOW DIGITS | FORMAT | BASE | ANGLE | SEED | ECHO
     SHOW MEMORY

   SHOW SETTINGS shows each setting as the SET command that makes it;
   SHOW and a setting's name shows that one. SHOW MEMORY counts the
   arrays and expression trees in use, which CLEAR ALL should bring to 0.

3 SAVE

     SAVE file

   Writes the settings, variables, and functions to the file, as fcalc's
   own commands: SET lines, assignments (each number with the fewest
   digits that read back exactly), and definitions. You can read and
   edit it. A file name with no type is given .fcalc.

3 LOAD

     LOAD file

   Clears every variable and function, then runs the file's lines
   quietly, as SAVE wrote them: the session SAVE saved, exactly.

3 @

     @file
     RUN file

   Runs the lines of a file of commands as if typed, with their output
   shown. A file may run another, up to 8 deep. An error ends the file,
   and every file that ran it, each saying the line it was at. EXIT or
   QUIT in a file ends that file only. A name with no type is given
   .fcalc.

3 LOG

     LOG file
     LOG OFF

   Keeps a transcript: each line typed, and everything fcalc shows, is
   added to the end of the file until LOG OFF.

3 HELP

     HELP [topic ...]

   Shows this help, the FCALC topic of the system's help library,
   SYS$HELP:HELPLIB.HLB: the same text as DCL's HELP FCALC. HELP COMMANDS
   SOLVE shows the topic SOLVE under Commands.

3 EXIT

     EXIT
     QUIT

   Ends fcalc, or the file of commands it is running. End of file
   (Ctrl/Z at a terminal) ends fcalc too.

2 Display

   Values are shown to 12 significant digits, in fixed notation from
   1e-5 to 1e11 and scientific notation outside it (SET FORMAT AUTO).
   Fixed notation drops trailing zeros: 1/4 is 0.25.

     SET DIGITS n          significant digits, 1 to 15 (12 unless set);
                           under FIXED, places after the point
     SET FORMAT FIXED      always fixed, with exactly DIGITS places
     SET FORMAT SCIENTIFIC always d.ddd...e+nn
     SET FORMAT ENGINEERING  scientific with the exponent a multiple of 3
     SET BASE HEX          whole numbers in hexadecimal, 0xFF; also OCTAL
                           (0o377), BINARY (0b11111111), and DECIMAL

   Under SET BASE, a value that isn't a whole number is shown in decimal.
   A part of a complex value smaller than the other by more than the
   digits shown is shown as zero, so 1e-20 + 1 is 1. An array's elements
   are shown to the digits of its largest.

   SHOW SETTINGS shows the settings as SET commands.

2 Messages

   An error is shown with the line, a ^ under the place it found, and a
   message in VMS's form:

     fcalc> 1 / (2 - 2)
              ^
     %FCALC-E-DIVZERO, division by zero

   An error (E) leaves the line with no value and ends a file of
   commands; a warning (W) gives the value and goes on. In a file, a
   second message says where:

     -FCALC-I-INFILE, in line 3 of setup.fcalc

   No input ends fcalc, and nothing it computes stops it: every operation
   checks its operands first, so that nothing overflows or traps.

2 Building

   fcalc's sources are in SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSHLP.EXAMPLES.FCALC], 16 files,
   each a Fortran 90 module but FCALC.F90, the main program. They are
   commented for a reader learning Fortran 90 and numerical programming.
   BUILDFCALC.COM, beside them, compiles each module (/G_FLOATING: fcalc
   needs G_floating's range), assembles and links them:

     $ @SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSHLP.EXAMPLES.FCALC]BUILDFCALC INSTALL

   builds FCALC.EXE there and, with INSTALL, copies it to SYS$SYSTEM. To
   change fcalc, copy the directory to one of your own and run the copy
   of BUILDFCALC.COM there; then define the symbol for your image:

     $ FCALC :== $DUA1:[ME.FCALC]FCALC

   govax's manual, chapter 14, describes fcalc as a program.

2 Examples

   Each topic below is a session, shown as a terminal shows it.

3 Arithmetic

     fcalc> 2^10 + 1
     1025
     fcalc> sqrt(2)
     1.41421356237
     fcalc> 1 / 3 + 1 / 6
     0.5
     fcalc> e^(i*pi)
     -1
     fcalc> (3 + 4i) / (1 - 2i)
     -1 + 2i
     fcalc> 20!
     2.43290200818e+18
     fcalc> set angle degrees
     fcalc> sin(30)
     0.5
     fcalc> atan2(1, -1)
     135
     fcalc> set base hex
     fcalc> 255 + 1
     0x100

3 Functions_of_your_own

     fcalc> f(x) = x^3 - 2*x - 5
     f(x) = x^3 - 2*x - 5
     fcalc> solve f(x) for x from 2
     x = 2.09455148154
     fcalc> diff f(x) for x at ans
     11.1614377265
     fcalc> fib(n) = if(n < 2, n, fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2))
     fib(n) = if(n < 2, n, fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2))
     fcalc> fib(15)
     610
     fcalc> list functions
     f(x) = x^3 - 2*x - 5
     fib(n) = if(n < 2, n, fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2))

3 Matrices

     fcalc> a = [4, -2; 1, 1]
     a =
       4  -2
       1   1
     fcalc> det(a)
     6
     fcalc> inv(a)
        0.166666666667  0.333333333333
       -0.166666666667  0.666666666667
     fcalc> eig(a)
       2
       3
     fcalc> a \ [2; 3]
       1.33333333333
       1.66666666667
     fcalc> v = range(1, 5)
     v =
       1  2  3  4  5
     fcalc> v .^ 2
       1  4  9  16  25
     fcalc> mean(v)
     3
     fcalc> stdev(v)
     1.58113883008

3 Numerics

     fcalc> integrate 4 / (1 + x^2) for x from 0 to 1
     3.14159265359
     fcalc> integrate sin(x) / x for x from 0 to pi
     1.85193705198
     fcalc> roots 1, 0, 0, -1
       -0.5 - 0.866025403784i
       -0.5 + 0.866025403784i
                            1
     fcalc> solve x = cos(x) for x from 0 to 1
     x = 0.739085133215

3 Table_and_plot

     fcalc> table x^2, sqrt(x) for x from 1 to 5 step 1
       x  x^2        sqrt(x)
       -  ---  -------------
       1    1              1
       2    4  1.41421356237
       3    9  1.73205080757
       4   16              2
       5   25   2.2360679775
     fcalc> plot sin(x), cos(x) for x from 0 to 2*pi size 50, 13 y -1, 1
      1 |oooo      ******                              oooo
        |    oo ***      **                          oo
        |     *oo          **                      oo
        |    *   o           **                   o
        |  **     oo           *                oo
        | *         o           *              o
      0 |*-----------o-----------**-----------o-----------*
        |             oo           *        oo           *
        |               o           *      o           **
        |                oo          **  oo           *
        |                  o           *o           **
        |                   ooo      ooo **      ***
     -1 |                      oooooo      ******
        +--------------------------------------------------
         0                                          6.28319
         * sin(x)   o cos(x)
